SoX Installation
----------------

SoX should compile and run on any POSIX compatible system.
To compile and install SoX with a default configuration,
run the following commands:

	./configure
	make
	make install

You will most probably need root's privileges to 'make install'
if you are installing into the default directory of /usr/local.

The configuration can be altered by creating 'configure.local';
see configure.example for what can be configured. You might want
to change the PREFIX, for example, to install somewhere else, or
set specific compiler options, or specify nonstandard directories
that you system might uses for headers and libraries.

Optional external libraries supporting extra audio encodings and
file formats (such as Ogg Vorbis) need to be installed before
compiling SoX itself. Currently, the following are supported:

AMRNB (dec+enc)	https://sourceforge.net/projects/opencore-amr/
AMRWB (WB dec)	https://sourceforge.net/projects/opencore-amr/
AMRVO (WB enc)	https://sourceforge.net/projects/opencore-amr/
FLAC		http://flac.sourceforge.net
LADSPA		http://www.ladspa.org
MAD MP3 (dec)	http://www.underbit.com/products/mad
LAME MP3 (enc)	http://lame.sourceforge.net
Ogg Vorbis	http://www.vorbis.com
Opus		http://www.opus-codec.org/
PNG		http://www.libpng.org/pub/png
Sndfile		https://github.com/libsndfile/libsndfile
WavPack		http://www.wavpack.com

Besides processing audio files off-line, SoX can play and record audio
if it recognizes and supports the underlying OS's audio subsystem.
The following are supported and autodetected by ./configure:

AO		http://xiph.org/ao
ALSA		https://www.alsa-project.org/wiki/Main_Page
sndio		https://sndio.org/
CoreAudio	https://developer.apple.com/documentation/coreaudio
OSS		http://www.opensound.com/oss.html
pulseaudio	https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/PulseAudio/

The ./configure script attempts to detect the libraries and drivers
present on your system. Read its standard output and the Makefile.local
file which it generates (and which the Makefile includes).
If anything looks wrong or different from what you wish,
read configure.example, create configure.local, and re-run
./configure until the result seems right to you.
You might also want to look at the config.h file
(which the sources files will eventualy #include),
or even the full log saved in config.log

After installing SoX, try to create, convert and play a sound file
as a test of basic functionality; for example:

	sox -n -b 16 file.wav synth 1 sin 220 sin 440 gain -3
	sox file.wav file.au
	play file.au

